# gateway.env — the WRITE credential (deployment spec §4; migrations/0003_two_roles.sql).
#
# Copy to ${OMEGAHIVE_SECRETS_DIR}/gateway.env (that dir is 0700; this file 0600) and fill in
# the real value THERE — never here, never anywhere in the repo tree. Names are declared in
# secrets-manifest.yaml (gateway row); scripts/credential_scope_scan.sh hard-fails on anything
# beyond them.
#
# One command does the copy with the right modes, for this and every other service:
#
#   scripts/hive-init-secrets            # then edit the seeded file
#
# WHICH SERVICES SEE THIS: cli, seed, coordinator, worker, review, deploy-checks, ladder —
# the services that emit, and no others. The read-only followers (ui, notifier, board-view)
# get only .env, which is why the read DSN is the strongest thing that may live there.
#
# UNTIL YOU WRITE THIS FILE nothing breaks: the compose entry is `required: false`, the
# variable stays unset, connect_gateway() falls back to the read DSN, and the deployment runs
# as it always has under one role. That is what makes the cutover reversible — delete this
# file and restart to go back.
#
# The role and its grants come from migration 0003; the PASSWORD does not (a migration in the
# repo must never carry a credential). Set it once, as the database owner:
#
#   podman compose run --rm --entrypoint sh backup \
#     -c 'psql "$OMEGAHIVE_OWNER_DATABASE_URL" -c "ALTER ROLE hive_gateway PASSWORD '"'"'…'"'"'"'
#
# Full procedure, including the reader and the rollback: RUNBOOK "Two-role cutover".

# hive_gateway's DSN — the ONLY credential in this deployment that can append to the spine.
# Host is the compose service name; the database is the same one .env names.
OMEGAHIVE_GATEWAY_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://hive_gateway:CHANGEME@postgres:5432/omegahive
